# === Root gitignore — applies across the whole AI-OS ===
# Keep this file as the single source of hygiene. Nested .gitignore files
# are only needed when a subdir has rules that genuinely can't live here.

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
Desktop.ini
._*

# Python
**/.venv/
**/__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
**/*.egg-info/

# Node
**/node_modules/
**/package-lock.json

# IDE
.vscode/
.idea/

# Env + secrets — credential-file patterns only (NOT broad folder names;
# MCP source code uses auth/ folders for authentication MODULES which must stay tracked)
**/.env
**/.env.local
**/token.json
**/credentials.json
**/*oauth*.json
**/*secret*.json
**/*.key
**/auth_link.html
spotify-cache.json
**/storage_state.json
**/cookies.json
.slack-mcp-tokens.json
# Playwright browser-auth cookie stores (session credentials per service)
mcps/playwright-mcp/auth/*.json

# Runtime artifacts (regenerated each session) — all logs are per-session, never tracked.
# (Was just hooks/pipeline-executor.log — broadened so MCP debug logs like
# mcps/**/mcp_server_debug.log don't accumulate diffs from an accidental commit.)
**/*.log
**/.DS_Store

# OAuth credentials NEVER tracked — operators copy oauth.json.template → oauth.json
# with their own Google Cloud Console credentials (which stay local).
mcps/google-workspace-mcp/oauth.json

# Claude Code config (operator-personal, per-machine — symmetric with vault/.obsidian/).
# Tool permissions, statusline, hooks config, MCP registrations live here per machine.
# The framework ships none of this; each operator's instance is their own source of truth.
.claude/

# Obsidian per-machine UI state — ONLY the volatile pane-layout files (they rewrite on every
# pane move / note open, so tracking them would churn constantly + conflict across machines;
# /aios:update also treats workspace.json as machine-local). The REST of vault/.obsidian/
# (app.json, plugins, hotkeys, appearance, graph layout, plugin data) IS tracked, so an
# operator's Obsidian setup is portable + syncs via the vault repo.
vault/.obsidian/workspace.json
vault/.obsidian/workspace-mobile.json

# AIOS Glass per-machine UI state — same species as workspace.json above, for the editor
# extension instead of Obsidian. Glass writes this at runtime; the framework ships none of it.
# shell.json holds font sizes, theme, locale, card visibility and workspaceFolders as ABSOLUTE
# paths; state.json holds folder sort orders, frequent-task counters and scratch session notes.
# None of it is portable to another machine, and tracking it churns on every font-size nudge —
# one such change is enough to show a dirty tree forever and train you to ignore git status.
.glass/

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Your own ignores go BELOW the marker line — `/aios:update` MERGES everything after
# it (it never overwrites your personal rules). Put private-data / machine-local
# patterns here (e.g. private financial reports, company-asset mirrors). For ignores
# that must never reach ANY clone (a secret, an OAuth cache), prefer .git/info/exclude
# (local-only — updates never touch it).
#
# ⚠️ AND THE CONVERSE, which is the easier mistake: anything you add ABOVE this marker
# is framework-owned territory and WILL BE REPLACED on the next `/aios:update` — the
# merge takes upstream's file wholesale above the marker and only re-appends your lines
# from below it. It happens silently, and the rule you lose is by definition one you
# thought was protecting something. If a pattern matters to you, it goes BELOW. If you
# believe a pattern belongs to the framework, contribute it upstream instead of pinning
# it locally, or the next sync quietly reverts you.
# ═══ AIOS-OPERATOR-IGNORES (keep this line; /aios:update preserves everything below it) ═══
